## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce cross-provider tagging policy across environments using plan JSON, with CI gating and explicit drift handling.

## Key Concepts
- plan JSON parsing across AWS and GCP
- per-env backends and state isolation
- gating deployments behind approvals
- drift detection via re-plan and re-validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js validator skeleton
const plan = require('./plan.json');
let ok = true;
(plan.resource_changes || []).forEach(r => {
  const tags = (r.change?.after?.tags) || {};
  if (!tags.CostCenter || !tags.Env) ok = false;
});
process.exit(ok ? 0 : 1);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would policy checks adapt to Terraform Cloud/Enterprise?
- How handle resources with dynamic tags or provider-specific nuances?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement policy-as-code governance for Terraform Cloud, ensuring prod safety beyond backend controls.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform Cloud and Sentinel governance
- Env-to-workspace mapping and state linkage
- CI integration with GitHub Actions for plan/apply gating
- Policy design distinguishing prod vs non-prod changes
- Local and CI testing of policies; post-merge audit of policy outcomes

## Code Example

```sentinel
import "tfplan/v2"

check = func(plan) {
  hasProdChange = false
  for _, rc in tfplan.resource_changes {
    if strings.contains(rc.address, "envs/prod/") {
      hasProdChange = true
      break
    }
  }
  // If prod resources are touched, deny unless approved in PR metadata
  if hasProdChange {
    return false
  }
  return true
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the policy to support time-bound approvals or multi-degree approvals?
- How would you validate policy effectiveness across 100s of PRs and detect gaps in coverage?
